  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "WvgfYB+gN6tfHeqLUYRHOasAWoA=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:20:33.629Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Before hash-first, every ref in document state was **server-attested** \u2014 the server computed the hash. Under hash-first refs are **client-claimed, server-verified**, and the design is explicit about what that does and does not change:\n\n- **The store is exactly as trustworthy as before.** Ingest verification means no sequence of client actions can cause wrong bytes to be served for a hash.\n- **The ref in document state is a claim until the upload commits.** A client that lies or has a bug produces a ref whose upload is rejected with `HashMismatch`, leaving a **dangling ref** \u2014 precisely the state an abandoned upload already produces, and one the architecture already tolerates.\n- **The dedup oracle is closed by default.** `reserve()` rejection (and `stat()` on an arbitrary hash) reveals whether given content exists on the server. That oracle already existed, and hash-first makes probing cheaper \u2014 so every endpoint revealing hash existence (`POST /attachments/reservations`, `GET`/`HEAD /attachments/:hash`) **requires upload-equivalent authorization by default**: probing for content is treated as privileged as adding content. The switchboard mounts all attachment routes behind the upload endpoint's authorization gate, and transports carry those credentials on fetch. The 409 body stays minimal \u2014 ref only, no file name, no size.\n- **A 409 must never include an existing `reservationId`.** Reservation ids are upload capabilities; leaking one across principals would let an attacker complete or poison someone else's in-flight upload.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor/docs/attachments-hash-first.md` \u2014 Trust model\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:20:33.629Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:20:33.629Z"
    }
  },
